  "text": "My first-author paper in Risks, with Prof. Eugene Pinsky. On a free feed of the ICE U.S. Dollar Index (the spot index, which can’t be traded directly), holding the dollar only overnight lost money for 15 years (Sharpe −1.36), and standard overfitting tests rated it robust. On the ICE Dollar Index futures contract from Barchart over the same 3,793 nights there was nothing there (+0.25, not significant).\n\nSpot and futures differ, so I didn’t stop there: an index rebuilt from the free feed’s own currency prices also showed nothing (+0.22), the gap opened overnight and closed by the next close (an open-price error, not carry), and Federal Reserve rates from FRED confirmed the closes. The cause was biased opening prices in the free feed. The second blind spot is an undeclared search: a strategy that looks strong with 6 variants reported doesn’t survive once all 216 tried are declared.\n\nMore: The project I’d walk you through first is the dollar paper. The free feed carries the ICE spot index; I compared it with ICE Dollar Index futures from Barchart and Federal Reserve rates from FRED. Spot and futures differ, so I also rebuilt the spot index from its six currencies (overnight Sharpe +0.22 against −1.36 on the free feed), ran a carry test and screened the opening prices: the error was in the recorded opens. I wrote a search engine that reproduces the original backtest on all 1,312 configurations about 17× faster, then reran the whole search 2,000 times against luck.\n\nThen the commodities work: 15 Bloomberg futures and 6 ETFs, where the same rotation rule gives opposite answers (ETF monthly winners $100 → $337 against $121 for equal weight). That paper is under review at Commodities.",
