  "text": "WikiFlow predicts which new Wikipedia editors will quit, from their first ten edits. About 60M revision events and 1.16M editors on a 5-node Dataproc cluster; 13 leakage-free features chosen from about 40 (I dropped co-editor graph features because they leaked the label). AUC 0.845 for a from-scratch logistic regression in 19 s, 0.909 for a distributed neural net in 86 s, and four newcomer archetypes from clustering. Published by Springer with Kunj Patel and Prof. Farshid Alizadeh-Shabdiz.\n\nMore: I kept 13 features from each editor’s first ten edits, out of about 40 candidates, and dropped co-editor graph features because they quietly leaked the answer. Clustering found four kinds of newcomer: casual contributors (402,276), frustrated burst editors (308,377), focused bot-like editors (307,231) and power users on talk pages (140,364). The from-scratch logistic regression trains in 19 seconds; the neural network adds 6.4 AUC points for 4.5× the time, which is the trade-off a team would actually weigh.",
