  "text": "I re-analysed a published 2026 benchmark on 101,766 diabetes encounters from 130 US hospitals. Under the same nested, patient-grouped cross-validation my LightGBM reached AUC 0.689 vs the published 0.664; the gain came from cohort construction and patient-history features. Then I turned it into a call list: phoning the riskiest 5% of discharges reaches 1,696 future readmissions, 3.0× what random calls reach. My subgroup audit found no gap by race or sex, but a drop from 0.73 to 0.63 across age bands.\n\nMore: The gain over the benchmark came from how I built the patient cohort and from patient-history features, not from a fancier model. Two Transformer models lost to gradient boosting at up to 65 times the compute, so the simpler model shipped. My fairness audit found no gap by race or sex but a drop from 0.73 to 0.63 across age bands, which argues for age-aware thresholds. The dashboard lets a care team pick how many calls it can make: the top 20% of discharges hold 40% of all readmissions.",
